{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015SNp7RTZnTBRDZXWJn7BLt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** go-unix-utils is a specification-driven experiment that derives formal specs from C source code, generates Go implementations via AI, and verifies parity against GNU reference binaries through differential testing. The goal is to find where the methodology breaks down.\n\n**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify) with shared infrastructure:\n- `cmd/` — per-utility Go implementations with differential tests\n- `pkg/testutils/` — differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` — Darwin/Linux syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` — shared formatting (columns, colors, sizes)\n- `magefiles/` — build targets (external, not a generation target)\n- `docs/` — human-authored specs (PRDs, use cases, test suites)\n\n**Current Implementation (from source code):** All of these packages have working code: pkg/testutils, pkg/sys (file metadata only), pkg/format (columns, colors, sizes), cmd/cat, cmd/wc, cmd/sponge, cmd/du, cmd/ls (core), cmd/version.\n\n**What remains:** The existing cmd/ls implements R1.2 (single-column), R1.3 (sort), R1.4 (hidden files), R2 (multi-arg), R3.1-R3.2, R5, R6.1-R6.2, R7.1 — but notably **R1.1 is not implemented**. Release 04.1 (ls extended, spec_complete) has no code. Releases 02.0, 02.1, 02.2 have pending specs (no PRDs — spec authoring is human work). Release 04.1 is the earliest release with completed specs and no code.\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\n- Releases 00.0, 01.1, 01.2, 01.3, 03.0, 04.0 — code exists (trust source_code over stale status fields)\n- Release 04.1 (ls extended) — spec_complete, code not started → **actionable**\n- Releases 02.0, 02.1, 02.2 — specs pending; spec authoring is human-owned (DD5) → blocked\n- R1.1 from prd008-ls is the only R1.x sub-requirement NOT implemented in cmd/ls; R1.2 is \"single-column output\"; R1.1 is almost certainly multi-column terminal output, which is precisely what pkg/format/column.go (Columns, PadRight) was built for\n- pkg/sys architecture lists \"Terminal size queries (TIOCGWINSZ ioctl)\" as a capability but no terminal.go exists — this is a prerequisite for multi-column ls\n\n## Proposed Task\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"prd008-ls R1.1: Implement multi-column terminal output with pkg/sys terminal width\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd008-ls.yaml (full ls PRD -- confirm R1.1 covers multi-column output and identify exact sub-requirements for terminal-aware column layout)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel04.1-uc001-ls-extended.yaml (extended ls use case defining the feature scope)\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-04.1.yaml (test suite for extended ls -- understand expected behavior)\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd002-sys.yaml (pkg/sys PRD -- terminal size query requirements)\n      - cmd/ls/main.go (existing ls implementation covering R1.2, R1.3, R1.4, R2, R3.1, R3.2, R5, R6.1, R6.2, R7.1)\n      - cmd/ls/ls_test.go (existing differential tests -- must remain passing)\n      - pkg/format/column.go (Columns and PadRight functions for multi-column layout computation)\n      - pkg/sys/fileinfo.go (existing pkg/sys coding pattern with golang.org/x/sys/unix and build tags)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/sys/terminal.go\n        action: create\n        note: TerminalWidth function querying terminal column count via TIOCGWINSZ ioctl\n      - path: cmd/ls/main.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Add multi-column output when stdout is a terminal plus -1 and -C flag support\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Add TerminalWidth(fd int) (int, error) to pkg/sys that returns the terminal column count via golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize with the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl. Return a non-nil error for non-terminal file descriptors.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"When stdout is a terminal, collect directory entry names into a slice and format them in multi-column layout using pkg/format.Columns(entries, termWidth), padding each non-final column entry with pkg/format.PadRight to match per-column widths. When stdout is not a terminal, preserve the existing one-per-line output (prd008-ls R1.2).\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Add -1 flag to force single-column (one entry per line) output regardless of terminal status, overriding multi-column default.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Add -C flag to force multi-column output regardless of terminal status, using a default width of 80 columns when TerminalWidth returns an error.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Use golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize in pkg/sys/terminal.go with the same //go:build darwin || linux tag as fileinfo.go. Unlike fileinfo.go which needs separate _darwin.go and _linux.go files for Stat_t field divergence, IoctlGetWinsize and Winsize have identical signatures on both platforms, so a single file with the shared build tag suffices.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Determine output mode in main() before processing arguments: if -1 is set use single-column, if -C is set use multi-column, otherwise call pkg/sys.TerminalWidth(int(os.Stdout.Fd())) -- if it returns an error stdout is not a terminal and single-column is used.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"For multi-column rendering, modify listDir to collect all filtered and sorted entry names into a []string first, then either call pkg/format.Columns(names, termWidth) to compute the grid and render each row with PadRight padding between columns, or fall through to one-per-line. The last entry in each row must not have trailing spaces. Preserve the existing entry filtering (-a, -A) and sort logic unchanged.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"pkg/sys.TerminalWidth compiles on both darwin and linux. It returns the column count for a terminal fd and a non-nil error for a non-terminal fd such as a pipe or regular file.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Running the compiled ls binary with stdout connected to a terminal produces multi-column output. Running with stdout piped (e.g. ls | cat) produces one-per-line output identical to the current behavior.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"The -1 flag forces one-per-line output even on a terminal. The -C flag forces multi-column output even when piped, defaulting to 80-column width.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"Existing differential tests in cmd/ls/ls_test.go continue to pass without modification. Tests run with piped stdout so they exercise the single-column code path and are unaffected by multi-column changes.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"The code compiles with no warnings under go vet. New code follows the project copyright header (Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic, SPDX MIT), package comment with PRD references, and error reporting patterns from the existing codebase.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":78197,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":78197},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b12deb80-b0fb-4cd2-b20f-e3db5ee23c08","uuid":"88166aa0-4d07-496a-9cc2-4f55018444ef"}
